BRIEF_BODY = '## 1. The one big idea\nAttention is a filter: when it\'s busy, unexpected things often don\'t reach awareness, even for experts, even outside the lab, and regardless of how clever you are. The 2025 twist: people who say they saw nothing often picked up more than they report, because they play it safe. So the gap is partly seeing and partly saying.\n\n**Open loops**\n\n| Loop | Opened | Closed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Would you really miss it? / Did they see nothing? | Slides 1-2 | Slides 19-21, 24 |\n| \'Hold that thought\' on the yes/no question | Slide 7 | Slides 17-21 |\n| Did the Boston officer lie? | Slide 11 | Slide 14 |\n| Is it just some people? | Slide 15 | Slide 16 |\n\n## 2. Basics plan (slides 4-7)\n\n| Concept | One plain sentence | Source | Number shown |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Attention as a filter | We can process only a small part of what reaches our senses; attention picks it. | Simons, Noba Project | None |\n| Inattentional blindness | Missing an unexpected thing because attention is busy elsewhere (the gorilla study). | Noba; Nartker intro | ~50% missed [S3] |\n| What makes you miss more | A harder task, and a surprise that resembles what you\'re ignoring. | Noba (citing Simons and Chabris 1999; Most et al. 2001) | ~30% missed a red cross [S8] |\n| How it\'s measured | Researchers ask one yes/no question; \'no\' is treated as seeing nothing. | Nartker et al. (2025) | None |\n\n## 3. Reports\n\n| ID | Source | Type | How fully read | Replication / status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | Drew, Võ and Wolfe, "The invisible gorilla strikes again: Sustained inattentional blindness in expert observers", Psychological Science 24(9), 2013 (doi 10.1177/0956797613479386) | Peer-reviewed | Full author manuscript (PMC PDF), plus PMC HTML | 13 years old; small sample (24). Widely cited; consistent with Potchen (2006). No retraction found. |\n| R2 | Chabris, Weinberger, Fontaine and Simons, "You do not talk about Fight Club if you do not notice Fight Club", i-Perception 2, 2011 (doi 10.1068/i0436) | Peer-reviewed short report | Full text (PMC PDF), plus PMC HTML | 15 years old; small samples (94 total). Real-world IB also reported by Hyman et al. (2010). |\n| R3 | Kreitz, Furley, Memmert and Simons, "Inattentional Blindness and Individual Differences in Cognitive Abilities", PLOS ONE 10(8), 2015 | Peer-reviewed, CC-BY | Full text (PLOS PDF), plus PMC HTML | Consistent with a 2026 registered report (Simons et al., Royal Society Open Science) reported by PsyPost; that paper could not be downloaded, so it is not used on screen. |\n| R4 | Nartker, Firestone, Egeth and Phillips, "Sensitivity to visual features in inattentional blindness", eLife, Version of Record 19 May 2025 (doi 10.7554/eLife.100337) | Peer-reviewed (eLife assessment), CC-BY | Full text (eLife PDF), plus PMC HTML | New; five pre-registered experiments; group-level analysis. |\n| B1 | Daniel Simons, "Failures of Awareness: The Case of Inattentional Blindness", Noba Project textbook module | Textbook chapter | Full module | Used for basics; survey figures in it are secondhand. |\n\n## 4. Research notes\n\n**R1 Drew et al. (2013).** Do experts show IB in their own domain? 24 radiologists searched five chest CT cases for nodules while eye-tracked; a gorilla 48 times the average nodule was inserted in the last case. 20 of 24 (83%) did not report it; 12 of those 20 looked directly at its location; untrained observers: none noticed; when cued, untrained observers detected it 88% of the time. Limitation: small sample, one image, gorilla dark against light targets (authors note a light gorilla might be noticed more).\n\n**R2 Chabris et al. (2011).** Does IB happen in real-world conditions? Students followed a runner and counted head-touches past a staged fight. Night 7/20 (35%) noticed; day 9/16 (56%); daytime high load 14/33 (42%) vs low load 18/25 (72%). Limitation: small samples, college students.\n\n**R3 Kreitz et al. (2015).** Do cognitive abilities predict who notices? Two studies (116 and 198 students). Working memory weakly predicted noticing in one task version only; attention breadth didn\'t; noticing in one task only weakly predicted another. Conclusion: not a stable trait. Limitation: student samples.\n\n**R4 Nartker et al. (2025).** Do \'non-noticers\' really see nothing? Five pre-registered online experiments, over 25,000 subjects, adding forced-choice feature questions and absent trials. Exp. 1: 71.4% said they noticed, 87.4% located the stimulus, non-noticers 63.6% correct (chance 50%). Conservative bias: few say yes when nothing is shown (7.2% in Exp. 5). Even high-confidence non-noticers above chance. Exp. 4: 57.3% still showed IB. Limitation: group-level analysis; online samples.\n\n**Synthesis.** The basics say attention filters awareness, worse under load and when the surprise resembles ignored items. R1 shows expertise doesn\'t protect (similarity explains the dark gorilla), R2 shows it outside the lab and confirms load, R3 shows it\'s situational rather than a personal trait, and R4 complicates the measurement: some of what we call blindness is cautious reporting. Clash: R4 vs the classic interpretation that IB means \'no awareness at all\'.\n\n'

